  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1219 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nSaturday begins the long term period with a 2nd Pacific storm system \ncrossing the northern tier of the state. An H3 100-120kt jetmax \nrounding this trough's southern tier will help it close off \nsomewhere over northern NM or just lee of the Rockies over \nnortheastern NM Saturday night. Combined with enough moisture and \nfavorable orographics along west facing slopes of the northern \nmountains, a quick 2 to 4 inches of snow will be favored in those \nareas by Sunday morning. Locally higher amounts upwards of 4 to 6 \ninches will favor the peaks of the Tusas Mountains. Forecast \nconfidence is rather high regarding this, as there is little spread \nin the NBM probabilities for snow amounts over the northern \nmountains. The difference b/w the 25th and 75th percentiles for snow \naccumulations is as little as 1 to 2 inches. Meanwhile, showers of \nrain will also favor the eastern plains of NM Saturday and Saturday \nnight.\n\nSnow levels look to start off around 9,500' Saturday, falling to \n8,500' by Sunday morning as lower elevations of central and northern \nNM look to receive rain from this system. Despite relatively light \nrain or liquid equivalent precipitation amounts ranging from 0.10\" \nto 0.50\", there still exists a low and minor (10% to 20%) risk of \nexacerbated flooding impacts through northwestern NM due to that \nareas still very moist ground conditions. \n\nPrecipitation looks to linger the longest over northeastern NM \nalongside breezy northerly to northeasterly winds as the H5 closed \nlow ejects out over the TX/OK Panhandles. Clearing conditions then \nfill in west to east over the state as the next ridge of high \npressure slides over the state by Monday. \n\nA more active weather pattern returns Tuesday into the middle of \nnext week with Pacific storm systems rolling into the western CONUS. \nThe next of which looks to bring another round of valley rain and \nhigh mountain snow to New Mexico sometime late Tuesday through early \nThursday. Various numerical model suites vary in their speed and \ntrack of this storm system over the region, specifically with how \nfast this system ejects northeastward over the state.",
